\section{The [[LearnlogRepo]] class}

The class needs four operations: running Git commands, initialising the
repository, beginning a run (committing the code state and the run
header), and finalising a run (committing the results).
We introduce the class shell here and fill in each method in its own
section below.

One run spans both of the last two operations, so the instance also
carries the identity of the run currently in progress: the run id that
links its two commits together, and the command it records.
[[finalize_run]] needs both, and an instance handles exactly one run
because each captured process creates its own [[LearnlogRepo]].

<<classes>>=
class LearnlogRepo:
    """Manages a ``.learnlog/`` Git repository.

    Uses ``--git-dir=.learnlog --work-tree=.`` so the student's
    working directory is the work tree.  The ``.learnlog/`` directory
    contains only Git internals.
    """

    def __init__(self, workdir):
        self.workdir = pathlib.Path(workdir)
        self.git_dir = self.workdir / GIT_DIR_NAME
        self.env = git_environment(
            self.git_dir, self.workdir,
        )
        self.run_id = None
        self.run_script = None
        self.run_args = []
        self.init_repo()

    <<repo methods>>
@

The constructor used to call [[init_repo]] only when [[.learnlog/]] was
missing.
Deciding from the directory alone is what a concurrent first run breaks
(\cref{sec:init-repo}): the directory appears the instant one process
starts initialising, so a second process constructed in that window
would conclude that a repository was ready and run its first
[[git add]] against one that was not there yet.
[[init_repo]] is idempotent and answers that question properly, so the
constructor simply always asks it.

The directory name appears in three places that must agree: the path we
hand to Git, the exclude pattern that keeps the log out of its own
history (\cref{sec:init-repo}), and the diagnostics file we write inside
it (\cref{sec:diagnostics}).
A constant makes that agreement checkable instead of a coincidence of
three string literals.

<<constants>>=
GIT_DIR_NAME = ".learnlog"
@


\section{The environment Git runs in}
\label{sec:git-environment}

Every Git command learnlog runs is told which repository to work on
through the environment rather than through arguments, so building
that environment is a decision of its own.
It used to be built by copying the process environment and overriding
[[GIT_DIR]] and [[GIT_WORK_TREE]] in it.
Overriding two variables answers the two questions those variables
ask, and leaves every other [[GIT_]] variable the parent process
exported pointing wherever it pointed.

That is not a hypothetical parent.
Git exports [[GIT_INDEX_FILE]] to the hooks it runs, so a project
whose [[pre-commit]] hook happens to run a logged program hands
learnlog's [[git add .]] the path of the \emph{student's own}
repository's index---and learnlog then stages the whole working
directory into it, replacing whatever the student had deliberately
staged.
The variables that name an object store
([[GIT_OBJECT_DIRECTORY]], [[GIT_ALTERNATE_OBJECT_DIRECTORIES]],
[[GIT_QUARANTINE_PATH]]) are the same class one level down: learnlog's
commits would be written into somebody else's store and vanish when it
is discarded.
So are [[GIT_AUTHOR_NAME]] and its relatives, which would attribute a
run record to whatever commit was being made when the hook fired, and
[[GIT_CONFIG]], which redirects the writes of [[git config]]---the
command that configures the repository below---into a file we were
never asked to touch.

The list of variables that must not survive is therefore open-ended,
and an override list can only ever name the ones we thought of.
Inverting it closes the question: strip every [[GIT_]] variable, then
add back the few that cannot decide what Git writes or where it goes.

Three groups earn a place on that list.
The transport variables---[[GIT_SSH]], [[GIT_SSH_COMMAND]],
[[GIT_SSH_VARIANT]], [[GIT_ASKPASS]], [[GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT]]---say
how the student reaches a remote, which is exactly what [[push]] and
[[pull]] need and exactly what a student is likely to have set in a
shell profile rather than in a config file; they run through this
environment (\cref{sec:push}), and stripping them would break the
sharing commands for everyone who configures Git that way.
The config-file selectors [[GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL]], [[GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM]]
and [[GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM]] say which files hold the student's
configuration, and learnlog's identity and signing policy is built on
reading it (\cref{sec:ensure-configured}): dropping them would make
learnlog commit under its fallback identity for everyone whose Git is
configured through those variables---containers, sandboxes, CI.
[[GIT_TRACE]] and its variants only ask Git to narrate what it is
doing, which is what somebody debugging learnlog's Git calls needs and
which changes no outcome, so a prefix test lets all of them through
without enumerating a moving target.

<<functions>>=
def git_environment(git_dir, workdir):
    """Return the environment learnlog runs Git commands in.

    Copies the process environment without any ``GIT_*`` variable
    that could redirect what Git writes, keeps the few that only
    describe how to reach a remote or which config files to read,
    and points Git at the learnlog repository.
    """
    env = {
        key: value
        for key, value in os.environ.items()
        if not key.startswith(GIT_VAR_PREFIX)
        or key in INHERITED_GIT_VARS
        or key.startswith(GIT_TRACE_PREFIX)
    }
    env["GIT_DIR"] = str(git_dir)
    env["GIT_WORK_TREE"] = str(workdir)
    return env
<<constants>>=
GIT_VAR_PREFIX = "GIT_"
GIT_TRACE_PREFIX = "GIT_TRACE"
INHERITED_GIT_VARS = frozenset({
    "GIT_SSH",
    "GIT_SSH_COMMAND",
    "GIT_SSH_VARIANT",
    "GIT_ASKPASS",
    "GIT_TERMINAL_PROMPT",
    "GIT_CONFIG_GLOBAL",
    "GIT_CONFIG_SYSTEM",
    "GIT_CONFIG_NOSYSTEM",
})
@

The environment is built once per [[LearnlogRepo]] and then handed to
every command, which is what lets [[run_index]] below say \enquote{this
index, for this run} by writing one key into it.
That only works because the key is not there to begin with: with the
parent's [[GIT_INDEX_FILE]] stripped, the variable is set in exactly
one place in this module, and one [[grep]] confirms it.

Let's verify that the variables that could redirect a write are gone
while the ones that describe the student's setup survive:

