\section{Repository initialisation}
\label{sec:init-repo}

On the very first run, we need to create the Git repository, configure
a committer identity, set up exclusion patterns, and make an initial
commit of all source files.

That first run need not be alone.
Two terminals open in the same project, a parallel [[make]], or a
logged program that starts another logged program all reach this method
within milliseconds of each other in a directory that has no
[[.learnlog/]] yet, and each of them finds no repository to use.
Running [[git init]] twice in \emph{sequence} is harmless---it is
deliberately re-runnable---but running it twice at once is not.
Both processes copy Git's template hooks into the new directory, and
the one that loses on any single file dies with \enquote{cannot copy
\ldots{} File exists}, taking its run with it.
Even where both survive, the initial commit does not: two commits made
with no [[HEAD]] to build on are two \emph{root} commits on the same
branch, and the branch update that lands second leaves the other root,
together with every run recorded on top of it, reachable from nothing.

The single-writer lock of \cref{sec:commit-lock} is the right mechanism
and cannot be used as it stands, because the file it locks lives inside
the very directory whose creation is being raced.
Creating that directory ourselves is what makes it usable: [[mkdir]]
with [[exist_ok]] is safe for any number of simultaneous callers, an
empty [[.learnlog/]] is one [[git init]] is perfectly happy to
initialise, and a lock file inside it is kept out of the log by the
same exclude pattern that keeps the rest of the Git directory out.

Under the lock, the question to ask is not whether the directory
exists---it does, we just made it---but whether the repository already
has a commit.
The loser of the race blocks until the winner's initial commit is in,
finds one, and returns without writing anything.

<<repo methods>>=
def init_repo(self):
    """Make sure the ``.learnlog/`` repository is ready to use.

    Creates the repository and makes an initial commit of all
    files when there is none.  Configures it (committer identity,
    signing, exclusion patterns) whether it created the
    repository or found one, so a cloned log is configured too.

    Creating is skipped when the repository already has a
    commit, so that concurrent first runs share one initial
    commit instead of making a root commit each.
    """
    if not self.has_commit():
        self.git_dir.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
        with self.commit_lock():
            <<create the repository unless another process got there first>>
    self.ensure_configured()
@

The check before the lock is the one that runs on every later
construction, and it is there to keep the common case---an established
repository---from queueing behind whatever else is committing.
The check inside the lock is the one that is authoritative.

<<create the repository unless another process got there first>>=
if not self.has_commit():
    self.git("init")
    self.write_configuration()
    <<initial commit>>
@

Creation and configuration used to be the same step, and this method
used to return as soon as it found a commit---which meant that a
repository learnlog did not create was never configured at all.
Every clone is such a repository (\cref{sec:clone}), and what it is
missing is not cosmetic: with no exclude file, the first run in the
clone stages the log repository into its own history, and with no
[[commit.gpgsign]] of its own, a student whose global configuration
turns signing on gets a [[GitError]] from every commit learnlog
attempts.
Neither had a repair path, because [[init]] took the same early
return.

Splitting the two questions is what fixes the whole family: creation
asks \enquote{does this repository have a commit?}, configuration asks
\enquote{does this repository have the settings learnlog needs?}, and
the second question has to be asked on every construction because its
answer can go stale under a repository that is perfectly established---
a clone that arrives without them, or a [[.learnlogignore]] the student
writes after the first run.
Creation calls [[write_configuration]] itself rather than leaving it
to the [[ensure_configured]] that follows, because the exclude file has
to be on disk \emph{before} the initial commit stages the tree---a
first commit made without it is a log that starts by swallowing the
student's virtual environment.

\enquote{Has a commit} is the property that matters, rather than
\enquote{has a Git directory}, because the directory appears at the
very start of initialisation and the repository only becomes usable at
the end of it.
The [[HEAD]] probe of \cref{sec:head-state} answers it: a tip that
resolves is a commit, and [[HEAD]] fails to resolve exactly while the
branch is still unborn---one of the places in this module where a
non-zero exit is the answer rather than a problem.
The filesystem answers for free in the one case where it can---a
directory that is not there cannot hold a commit---but every
[[LearnlogRepo]] built on an existing log does pay for one probe.
That is the price of the correctness above, and it is a small one next
to what the same process is about to do: a run makes two commits, each
of which walks the work tree.

<<repo methods>>=
def has_commit(self):
    """Return whether the repository exists and has a commit."""
    if not self.git_dir.is_dir():
        return False
    tip, _ = self.head_state()
    return tip is not None
@

Where [[fcntl]] is missing the lock is a no-op
(\cref{sec:commit-lock}), and check-then-initialise is racy again.
That is the same trade as for the commit lock itself: a platform
without [[flock]] gets the protocol without the serialisation, rather
than no learnlog at all.

\subsection{Keeping the repository configured}
\label{sec:ensure-configured}

A configured learnlog repository is one where three things hold: Git
can resolve a committer identity, [[commit.gpgsign]] is off, and
[[info/exclude]] lists the patterns that keep the log out of its own
history.
[[ensure_configured]] establishes all three, and it has to be safe to
call on every construction, because that is when the answers can have
changed since the last one.

It asks before it writes, and takes the lock only when the answer is
no.
Taking it unconditionally would put every program start in the queue
behind whatever commit is in progress, which is the cost
\cref{sec:commit-lock} refuses to pay for a lock that only means
\enquote{learnlog is writing}.
The questions are read-only, and the writes they lead to are the same
writes any other process would make from the same answers, so a lost
race costs a second write of identical content and never a wrong one.

<<repo methods>>=
def ensure_configured(self):
    """Bring the repository's configuration up to date.

    Does nothing when the settings and exclude patterns learnlog
    needs are already in place, so it is cheap to call on every
    construction and repairs whatever it finds missing.
    """
    if self.is_configured():
        return
    with self.commit_lock():
        self.write_configuration()
@

Asking costs one [[git config --list]] probe on top of the [[HEAD]]
probe of [[has_commit]], which is the honest price of not trusting a
marker.
A marker saying \enquote{this repository was configured once} is in
effect what the old early return believed, and a clone or a
[[.learnlogignore]] written after the first run is exactly the case
where that belief is wrong.
Listing is barely more work for Git than asking for one key---either
way it parses every config file in scope---and for us the difference
is a whole process: this method used to ask twice, once per setting,
and each ask costs a fork and an exec before Git reads a byte.
One listing answers both questions, and would answer a third for
free if configuration ever grows one.

The exclude file is checked first because it is answered by a file
read rather than by a process, and because it is the setting most
likely to be stale: it changes whenever the student edits
[[.learnlogignore]].

<<repo methods>>=
def is_configured(self):
    """Return whether our settings are already in place."""
    try:
        current = (self.git_dir / EXCLUDE_PATH).read_text()
    except OSError:
        return False
    if current != self.exclude_content():
        return False
    listing = self.git("config", "--list", "-z", check=False)
    if listing.returncode != 0:
        return False
    settings = parse_config_listing(listing.stdout)
    if "user.name" not in settings:
        return False
    return _config_bool(settings.get("commit.gpgsign")) is False
@

The probe is among the places in this module where a non-zero exit is
the answer rather than a problem, so it runs with [[check=False]]: a
repository whose configuration cannot even be listed is one we should
configure, not one to fail on.

Reading the listing ourselves means taking over two things
[[git config <key>]] did for us: picking which of a key's
occurrences wins, and reading booleans the way Git spells them.
Git prints the listing in scope order---system, then global, then
local---and resolves a single-valued key to the occurrence read last,
so a dictionary that lets later entries overwrite earlier ones gives
the same answer.
The NUL framing ([[-z]]) is not decoration: a config value can
contain newlines, so the newline that separates a key from its value
is only trustworthy inside a NUL-delimited entry.
An entry with no newline at all is a key set without [[=]], which for
a boolean means true---so it must stay distinguishable from an empty
value, which means false.

<<functions>>=
def parse_config_listing(output):
    """Map config keys to values from ``git config --list -z``.

    A later occurrence of a key overrides an earlier one, matching
    how Git resolves a single-valued key across scopes.  A key set
    without a value maps to `None`.
    """
    settings = {}
    for entry in output.split("\0"):
        if not entry:
            continue
        key, separator, value = entry.partition("\n")
        settings[key] = value if separator else None
    return settings
@

Git accepts more spellings of a boolean than [[true]] and [[false]],
and students' global config files use them, so the reader has to
know the same vocabulary.
Anything outside it reads as \enquote{neither}, which
[[is_configured]] treats as not configured: the write that follows
replaces the unreadable value with one this reader understands, which
is the repairing direction.

<<functions>>=
def _config_bool(value):
    """Read a config value as Git reads booleans.

    Returns `True` or `False` for the spellings Git accepts, and
    `None` for anything else.  A key set without a value (`None`)
    is true, an empty value false.
    """
    if value is None:
        return True
    text = value.strip().lower()
    if text in ("yes", "on", "true", "1"):
        return True
    if text in ("no", "off", "false", "0", ""):
        return False
    return None
@

Let's verify the two things the parse took over from
[[git config <key>]]: that a later occurrence of a key wins, and
that a value containing a newline---or no value at all---survives
the framing.

<<test functions>>=
def test_parse_config_listing_last_occurrence_wins():
    """A local value overrides a global one, as Git resolves it."""
    output = (
        "user.name\nGlobal\0"
        "commit.gpgsign\ntrue\0"
        "user.name\nLocal\0"
        "commit.gpgsign\nfalse\0"
    )
    settings = parse_config_listing(output)
    assert settings["user.name"] == "Local"
    assert settings["commit.gpgsign"] == "false"

def test_parse_config_listing_awkward_values():
    """Newlines stay in values; a bare key maps to None."""
    output = "alias.lg\nlog\n--graph\0commit.gpgsign\0"
    settings = parse_config_listing(output)
    assert settings["alias.lg"] == "log\n--graph"
    assert settings["commit.gpgsign"] is None

def test_config_bool_reads_gits_spellings():
    """The boolean vocabulary matches Git's, case-insensitively."""
    from learnlog.gitrepo import _config_bool
    assert _config_bool(None) is True
    for text in ("yes", "On", "TRUE", "1"):
        assert _config_bool(text) is True
    for text in ("no", "Off", "FALSE", "0", ""):
        assert _config_bool(text) is False
    assert _config_bool("maybe") is None
@

