An identity the student configured globally is the right one for a log
of \emph{their} work, so learnlog only supplies a fallback when Git can
resolve none---which is the lab machine where nobody ever ran
[[git config]].
Signing is the mirror image: whatever the student's global
configuration says, these machine-generated commits must not need a
key, so learnlog writes the local [[false]] that overrides it.
Reading the effective value rather than the local one is what makes
the write happen once instead of on every run.

<<repo methods>>=
def write_configuration(self):
    """Write the Git settings and exclude patterns we need."""
    identity = self.git("config", "user.name", check=False)
    if identity.returncode != 0:
        self.git(
            "config", "user.name", FALLBACK_USER_NAME,
        )
        self.git(
            "config", "user.email", FALLBACK_USER_EMAIL,
        )
    self.git("config", "commit.gpgsign", "false")
    <<swap the exclude file into place>>
<<constants>>=
FALLBACK_USER_NAME = "learnlog"
FALLBACK_USER_EMAIL = "learnlog@localhost"
@

The exclude file ([[.learnlog/info/exclude]]) keeps infrastructure
files out of the log: [[.learnlog]] itself, [[__pycache__]] and
compiled bytecode, virtual environments, and the files
[[learnlog export]] writes.
If the student has a [[.learnlogignore]] file, its patterns are
appended---this is the only configuration hook, and it is empty by
default.

The first pattern carries more weight than it looks: it is the only
thing standing between the Git directory and its own history.
Git ignores a directory named [[.git]] by rule, but [[.learnlog]] is an
ordinary directory to [[git add]], so anything we write inside it---the
diagnostics log of \cref{sec:diagnostics}, a lock file, a session
transcript---is excluded by this line and by nothing else.
Naming it from [[GIT_DIR_NAME]] keeps the pattern and the path
inseparable.

The export patterns are there for a mistake that compounds.
[[learnlog export]] writes its bundle into the directory it is run
from, which is normally the work tree (\cref{sec:export}), so without
these two lines the next run commits the bundle---and the run after
that exports a bundle containing the previous bundle.
Each cycle roughly doubles what the log carries, from a file the log
already contains in full.

The two export extensions are named here rather than imported from the
modules that write them---[[cli]] and [[progsnap2]] both import this
one, so the dependency only goes this way---and a test in [[cli]],
which knows both default names, asserts that they still agree with
these suffixes (\cref{sec:staged-export}).

<<constants>>=
BUNDLE_SUFFIX = ".learnlog"
PROGSNAP2_SUFFIX = ".progsnap2"
EXCLUDE_PATH = "info/exclude"
EXCLUDE_PATTERNS = (
    GIT_DIR_NAME,
    "__pycache__",
    "*.pyc",
    ".venv",
    "venv",
    "env",
    "*" + BUNDLE_SUFFIX,
    "*" + PROGSNAP2_SUFFIX,
)
LEARNLOGIGNORE_NAME = ".learnlogignore"
@

Deriving the file's whole content from the patterns, rather than
appending to whatever is there, is what makes the write idempotent:
running it twice leaves the same file, and comparing the two answers
the question [[is_configured]] asks.
It also means a [[.learnlogignore]] the student edits at any point
takes effect on the next run instead of being frozen into the file the
first run wrote.

<<repo methods>>=
def exclude_content(self):
    """Return the text the exclude file should hold."""
    patterns = list(EXCLUDE_PATTERNS)
    try:
        extra = (
            self.workdir / LEARNLOGIGNORE_NAME
        ).read_text()
    except FileNotFoundError:
        extra = ""
    patterns.extend(extra.splitlines())
    return "\n".join(patterns) + "\n"
@

The new content is written beside the exclude file and moved onto it,
because [[git add .]] reads this file and not every reader is one we
serialise: [[learnlog git]] hands the student Git itself
(\cref{sec:git}), and a half-written exclude file is a file that
excludes nothing.
[[os.replace]] within one directory is atomic, so a reader sees either
the old list or the new one.

<<swap the exclude file into place>>=
exclude_file = self.git_dir / EXCLUDE_PATH
exclude_file.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)
staged = exclude_file.with_name(
    exclude_file.name + STAGED_SUFFIX
)
staged.write_text(self.exclude_content())
os.replace(staged, exclude_file)
<<constants>>=
STAGED_SUFFIX = ".new"
