\section{One run, two commits}
\label{sec:two-commit-protocol}

A run produces information at two different moments: what the student
was about to run---the command, its arguments, and the code as it
looked right then---and how it went---the end time, the exit code, the
captured I/O, and any exception.
Writing the first half down \emph{before} the program starts is what
makes the log crash-resilient: a program killed by [[SIGKILL]] never
reaches our exit handler, but its header is already committed.

Earlier versions completed such a record with [[git commit --amend]].
That is only correct while learnlog is the sole writer and nobody has
seen the commit yet, and neither assumption holds:

\begin{description}
\item[Overlapping runs] [[--amend]] rewrites whatever sits at
  \emph{HEAD}, not the commit this run created.  A program that starts
  another logged program, a run left in the background, or a
  [[learnlog]] command overlapping a student run all end with a
  different commit at HEAD, so the results are appended to a stranger's
  run block: one run looks like it crashed, the other reports someone
  else's output.
\item[Published history] Amending replaces a commit that
  [[learnlog push]] may already have sent and [[learnlog tag]] may
  already point at.  The next push is rejected as non-fast-forward, and
  the tag is left behind on an abandoned commit.
\item[Timestamps] An amend keeps the original author date, so runs
  folded into one commit all share the first run's timestamp, and the
  interval between runs (\cref{commits}) is wrong for every run but
  that one.
\end{description}

So we never rewrite anything.
[[begin_run]] writes a \emph{header commit} carrying a fresh run id,
and [[finalize_run]] writes a separate \emph{trailer commit} that
repeats that run id and carries the results.
Each process only ever \emph{adds} a commit, and it names the run it is
talking about instead of trusting HEAD, which is what makes the
protocol safe under any interleaving.

Folding consecutive runs of unchanged code into one commit goes away
with the amend that implemented it: every run now gets its own header
commit, even when it records no change at all.
Empty commits cost a few dozen bytes---the tree is shared---and in
exchange a run's record no longer depends on what the previous run
wrote.

The price is that [[learnlog git log]] shows two commits per run.
Every learnlog reader---[[list]], [[play]], and the ProgSnap2
exporter---joins the pair back into a single record by run id
(\cref{commits}), so the two-commit structure is visible only to
someone inspecting the Git history directly.


\subsection{Which code state belongs to a run}

The header commit also decides \emph{when} the code is snapshotted, and
that is a second thing the old protocol got wrong: it staged the work
tree at the end of the run, so a program that wrote [[a.out]] committed
that binary into its own run.

The code state that explains a run is the code that was executed, not
what the execution produced.
We therefore snapshot at the start and leave the tree alone afterwards.
Files written during the run are not lost: the next run's snapshot
picks them up, where they appear as a change that happened between the
two runs---which is exactly what they are.
If the student never runs anything again, those files never enter the
log, and a build artefact is no loss.


\subsection{Run identity}

A run id has to be unique within one repository, and it is minted by
processes that cannot see each other---the whole point is that they do
not coordinate---so a counter is not an option.
A random UUID is: 122 random bits make a collision far less likely than
a disk error.

<<functions>>=
def new_run_id():
    """Return a fresh run identifier.

    The id links a run's header commit to its trailer commit.
    """
    return uuid.uuid4().hex
@


\subsection{Serialising commits}
\label{sec:commit-lock}

Two learnlog processes can reach [[begin_run]] at the same instant.
Git guards its own data structures with lock files, but a losing
command \emph{fails} ([[index.lock]] already exists) rather than
waiting, and a dropped run is data loss.
We would rather wait a few milliseconds, so we take an exclusive
[[flock]] on a file in [[.learnlog/]] around each commit.

The lock covers one commit operation and nothing more.
A lock held from [[begin_run]] to [[finalize_run]] would make two
terminals take turns \emph{running programs} instead of taking turns
\emph{committing}---a student's program would block on another
student's program, which breaks transparency far worse than the race it
prevents.

Repository creation takes this same lock (\cref{sec:init-repo}),
because the initial commit is a commit that must happen exactly once
too.
The lock therefore means \enquote{learnlog is writing to this
repository}, not \enquote{a run is being committed}, and that is why
its file has to live somewhere creation can be relied on to have made:
inside [[.learnlog/]], which [[init_repo]] creates before it takes the
lock.

[[fcntl]] does not exist on Windows.
Rather than fail there, we degrade to no locking: the protocol is still
append-only, and the remaining window is the one Git itself guards.

<<optional locking support>>=
try:
    import fcntl
except ImportError:  # pragma: no cover - Windows has no fcntl
    fcntl = None
@

<<repo methods>>=
@contextlib.contextmanager
def commit_lock(self):
    """Hold an exclusive lock while writing one commit.

    Yields the locked handle, or ``None`` where no lock could be
    taken and the commit therefore proceeds unserialised.
    """
    handle = self._acquire_lock()
    try:
        yield handle
    finally:
        if handle is not None:
            handle.close()
@

The handle comes back out of the context manager because one caller has
to know whether the exclusion it is standing on is real
(\cref{sec:reclaim-run-indexes}).
For every other caller the answer changes nothing---a commit is made
either way---but an operation that is only safe while nobody else is
committing cannot treat \enquote{locked} and \enquote{could not lock}
as the same situation, and [[None]] is how this method says which one
happened.

Closing the file releases the lock, which also means the kernel releases
it if the process dies mid-commit---no stale lock can wedge the next
run.
Failing to acquire the lock is not worth aborting a run over, so every
error path here returns [[None]] and the commit proceeds unserialised.

Which file, though?
[[flock]] locks the open file---the inode---and the path is only how we
found it.
The two mean the same thing for exactly as long as nobody replaces the
file behind the name.
Delete [[learnlog.lock]] while a run holds it and the name is free
again: the next process opens the path, creates a new file there, and
takes an exclusive lock on \emph{that} one.
Two processes now hold the lock, on two different files, and the kernel
is right about both.

Nobody deletes that file on purpose, but plenty of housekeeping does.
A run killed between [[git add]] and its commit leaves behind the
[[.lock]] file Git was writing its private index through, and the
standard advice for a stray [[.lock]] in a Git repository is to delete
it; a student who follows that advice with a wildcard takes
[[learnlog.lock]] along with it.

The process that suffers is not the one holding the lock---it holds a
lock on a file, and it finishes its commit---but the one that was
\emph{waiting} inside [[flock]] while the file was replaced.
It went to sleep on the old file and wakes up holding a lock nobody
will ever contend for, because everybody else now opens the new one.
So once [[flock]] returns we ask whether the file we locked is still
the file the lock path names, and when it is not we drop it and start
over.
Starting over opens the path again---by then the new file---and waits
for whoever holds that one, which is the serialisation we asked for in
the first place.

The attempts are bounded because what they wait on is somebody else's
behaviour: something recreating the file in a loop would otherwise keep
us here for as long as it runs, and a student's program would wait for
it.
Running out of attempts is answered like any other failure to
acquire---[[None]], and the commit proceeds unserialised.

<<repo methods>>=
def _acquire_lock(self):
    """Return a locked file handle, or ``None`` if unavailable."""
    if fcntl is None:
        return None
    lock_path = self.git_dir / LOCK_FILE_NAME
    for _ in range(LOCK_ATTEMPTS):
        try:
            handle = open(lock_path, "w")
            fcntl.flock(handle.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_EX)
        except OSError:
            return None
        if _is_same_file(handle, lock_path):
            return handle
        handle.close()
    return None
<<constants>>=
LOCK_FILE_NAME = "learnlog.lock"
LOCK_ATTEMPTS = 8
@

Inode and device number are what \enquote{the same file} means to the
kernel, and they are the terms [[flock]] is itself defined in.
Comparing paths would answer a question we already know the answer to,
and comparing contents would answer none---the file is empty either
way.
A [[stat]] that fails means there is no file at the path at all just
now, which is a mismatch like any other.

<<functions>>=
def _is_same_file(handle, path):
    """Say whether ``handle`` is open on the file now at ``path``."""
    try:
        held = os.fstat(handle.fileno())
        named = os.stat(path)
    except OSError:
        return False
    return (held.st_ino, held.st_dev) == (named.st_ino, named.st_dev)
@

Let us replace the file underneath an acquisition and check that what
comes back is a lock on the file the next process would open---and that
the next process therefore cannot have it.
Driving [[_acquire_lock]] directly, with a stand-in for [[flock]] that
swaps the file just before it locks, turns the race into a sequence
instead of a coincidence.
The tests skip where [[fcntl]] does, for the same reason the module
degrades there.

<<test functions>>=
fcntl = pytest.importorskip("fcntl")

def test_lock_survives_a_recreated_lock_file(workdir, monkeypatch):
    """A lock file swapped mid-acquisition still excludes rivals."""
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    lock_path = repo.git_dir / LOCK_FILE_NAME
    real_flock = fcntl.flock
    swapped = []

    def swap_the_file_then_lock(fd, operation):
        if not swapped:
            swapped.append(True)
            lock_path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
            lock_path.write_text("")
        return real_flock(fd, operation)

    monkeypatch.setattr(fcntl, "flock", swap_the_file_then_lock)
    handle = repo._acquire_lock()
    monkeypatch.undo()
    assert handle is not None
    assert os.fstat(handle.fileno()).st_ino == lock_path.stat().st_ino
    with open(lock_path) as rival:
        with pytest.raises(BlockingIOError):
            fcntl.flock(rival.fileno(), fcntl.LOCK_EX | fcntl.LOCK_NB)
    handle.close()
@

And when the file never stops moving, the loop has to give up rather
than hold a student's program hostage to whatever keeps moving it:

