def test_lock_gives_up_on_a_file_that_keeps_moving(workdir, monkeypatch):
    """An endlessly recreated lock file ends in no lock, not a hang."""
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    lock_path = repo.git_dir / LOCK_FILE_NAME
    real_flock = fcntl.flock

    def swap_the_file_then_lock(fd, operation):
        lock_path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        lock_path.write_text("")
        return real_flock(fd, operation)

    monkeypatch.setattr(fcntl, "flock", swap_the_file_then_lock)
    assert repo._acquire_lock() is None
@


\subsection{A private index per run}
\label{sec:private-index}

Staging with [[git add .]] writes the repository's shared index.
Two concurrent runs sharing that file do not merely collide on
[[index.lock]]: the loser may also snapshot the winner's staged state,
so one run's commit would carry another run's tree.
Pointing [[GIT_INDEX_FILE]] at a per-run file makes each snapshot
private, and Git then has nothing left to contend on.

We set the variable on the environment dict that [[git]] passes to every
command rather than threading a parameter through it, so the wrapper
stays a plain Git runner.
A [[LearnlogRepo]] instance serves a single run, so no two commits share
this dict.

Two files come off again when the commit is done, and it is the second
one that makes this more than tidiness.
Git writes an index the way it writes everything it must not corrupt:
it creates the index's own name with [[.lock]] appended, builds the new
index in that file, and renames it into place.
A private index therefore has a private lock file beside it for as long
as Git is writing it, and a run killed in that window---the window a
[[git add .]] over a large project holds open the longest---leaves the
pair behind.

An index left behind on its own would be harmless: the name carries the
run id, so nothing ever reuses it, and Git ignores every file in its
directory that it did not ask for.
Its [[.lock]] is not harmless, and what makes it dangerous is not what
it does but what it looks like.
A stray [[.lock]] file is the one piece of Git housekeeping every
student eventually meets, and what they are told to do with it is
delete it; done with a wildcard over the directory it takes
[[learnlog.lock]] along with it, which is precisely the accident
\cref{sec:commit-lock} goes to some trouble to survive.
Surviving the trap is worth having, but leaving the bait out is not.

<<repo methods>>=
@contextlib.contextmanager
def run_index(self, name):
    """Point Git at a private index file named after the run."""
    index_path = self.git_dir / f"{RUN_INDEX_PREFIX}{name}"
    self.env["GIT_INDEX_FILE"] = str(index_path)
    try:
        yield index_path
    finally:
        self.env.pop("GIT_INDEX_FILE", None)
        <<remove the private index and its lock file>>
