\section{Where a run's commits land}
\label{sec:recording-branch}

Both commits of \cref{sec:two-commit-protocol} were made with
[[git commit]], and that single choice handed Git two decisions this
module has to make for itself.

\subsection{A half-finished merge is not ours to finish}
\label{sec:unfinished-operations}

Learnlog is not the only writer of the log repository.
The student drives it directly through [[learnlog git]] (\cref{cli}),
and does so most often when moving between machines: pull, and---when
both machines recorded runs---merge.
A merge that stops on a conflict leaves the repository half-finished:
[[MERGE_HEAD]] names the other side, the index holds every conflicted
path in two or three versions at once, and Git waits for the student
to conclude or abandon the operation.

[[git commit]] is the command that concludes it.
That is documented behaviour and students depend on it; it is simply
not what a recorder that happens to fire in that window wants.
Such a run committed the work tree---conflict markers and all---as a
two-parent merge commit, cleared [[MERGE_HEAD]], and left the student
holding a merge Git now considered finished with a resolution nobody
had chosen.
Cherry-pick, revert and rebase park their state in the same place and
came apart in the same way; the cherry-pick even lent the run header
the author date of the commit being picked, so the record was
timestamped wrong as well.

What fixes this is the mechanism rather than a check.
[[git commit-tree]] builds a commit from a tree and the parents we
name, so there is no rule by which it could adopt [[MERGE_HEAD]] as a
second parent, and it cleans nothing up afterwards.
The student's half-finished operation survives the run exactly as they
left it.

A rebase is the honest limit of this, because it is the operation that
rewrites the branch rather than adding to it: commits a run makes
while one is in progress land on the branch as it stands, and the
rebase then replaces that branch with its own result.
The run record is lost and the student's rebase is not, which is the
right way round---a recorder that survived by interfering is the
failure this section is about.

\subsection{A run belongs to the branch, not to [[HEAD]]}

Git's other decision is where the commit goes: [[git commit]] moves
whatever [[HEAD]] points at.
That is right for a person and wrong for us, because [[HEAD]] is
another thing the student can move.
[[learnlog git checkout HEAD~4]] is a natural way to look at what a
program used to be, and it detaches [[HEAD]].
Runs recorded afterwards piled onto that detached head: the branch
never moved, so nothing that reads the branch saw them; and since
[[learnlog push]] sends [[refs/heads/*]], it pushed none of them.
The only thing keeping them alive was the reflog.

[[git commit-tree]] moves no ref at all.
We move one ourselves, by name, with [[git update-ref]], and the name
is the branch the log is recorded on rather than wherever [[HEAD]]
happens to be pointing.
Re-attaching [[HEAD]] would have been the other way to make the branch
move again, and we deliberately do not: the student asked to stand at
an older commit, and a recorder that undoes an explicit checkout is
interfering with the very work it exists to observe.
A run made from a detached head is recorded on the branch, [[HEAD]]
stays where the student put it, and the two facts are independent.

\subsection{Which branch that is}

Ordinarily it is the branch [[HEAD]] points at, and
[[git symbolic-ref]] names it in full.
Asking per commit rather than remembering the answer is what lets a
student who works on a branch of their own have their runs recorded
there, which is what making a branch asks for.

When [[HEAD]] is detached there is no such answer, so we ask the refs
instead: among the repository's branches, the one committed to most
recently is the one the log has been growing on, because a log grows
one run at a time and nothing else commits here.
Learnlog never makes a second branch---[[init]] and [[clone]] leave
exactly one, whatever it is called---so the sort only has to choose
between branches the student made.
A repository with no branch left at all is past guessing; there we
fall back to [[HEAD]] and record the run where the old code did.

This is also why the branch is discovered rather than assumed: a
[[.learnlog]] made by [[git init]] and one made by [[git clone]] from
a classmate's remote need not agree on what the branch is called, and
neither has to match this machine's [[init.defaultBranch]].

