\subsection{Adding one commit to a branch}
\label{sec:commit-on-branch}

Three commands replace the one: a [[rev-parse]] reads the tip the new
commit will build on, [[commit-tree]] writes the commit object, and
[[update-ref]] moves the branch to it.

Naming the parent turns the append-only protocol of
\cref{sec:two-commit-protocol} from something inherited into something
stated---and, more usefully, into something checkable.
[[git update-ref]] takes the value the ref is expected to still have
and refuses to move it otherwise.
Every learnlog commit holds the lock of \cref{sec:commit-lock}, so the
only writer that guard can catch is the student's own Git: a
[[learnlog git commit]] or [[learnlog git pull]] landing between our
two commands.
Losing a run to that would be the wrong answer, so we read the tip
again and rebuild the commit on top of whatever they wrote.

The [[rev-parse]] is usually not needed: both run commits already
hold the tip from the probe of \cref{sec:head-state}, so the caller
may pass it as [[parent]] and the first attempt builds on it
directly.
The guarded [[update-ref]] is what makes accepting a caller's value
safe---a hint that has gone stale fails the update exactly like a
lost race does, and the retry discards it and reads the ref itself.
The default is a sentinel rather than [[None]] because [[None]] is
taken: it is how a caller says the branch must not exist yet, which
no hint may be mistaken for.

<<repo methods>>=
def commit_on_branch(self, branch, tree, message,
                     parent=RESOLVE_PARENT):
    """Add a commit holding ``tree`` to the end of ``branch``.

    ``tree`` may be any tree-ish; it is resolved afresh on each
    attempt, so a caller may name it relative to the branch.
    ``message`` becomes the commit message verbatim.  ``parent``
    is the tip the caller believes the branch is at (`None` for a
    branch that must not exist yet); leave it unset to have each
    attempt read the tip from the repository.  Returns the new
    commit's hash.
    """
    if not message.endswith("\n"):
        message += "\n"
    for _ in range(BRANCH_UPDATE_ATTEMPTS):
        <<build one commit and try to move [[branch]] to it>>
    raise GitError(update, moved.returncode, moved.stderr)
<<constants>>=
BRANCH_UPDATE_ATTEMPTS = 3
RESOLVE_PARENT = object()
@

Re-resolving [[tree]] on every attempt is what makes the retry safe
for a caller that names a tree relative to the branch: the trailer of
\cref{sec:finalising-a-run} wants \emph{the tree at the end of the
log}, and after a lost race that is a different tree than it was.
A caller passing a concrete hash gets the same hash each time, so the
two kinds of caller need no second method.

An attempt that exhausts the retries raises rather than returning
quietly, because the caller is a run that has just been recorded
half-way; \cref{sec:diagnostics} is where that ends up.

<<build one commit and try to move [[branch]] to it>>=
if parent is RESOLVE_PARENT:
    parent = self.branch_tip(branch)
commit = self.git(
    "commit-tree", tree, *(("-p", parent) if parent else ()),
    input=message,
).stdout.strip()
update = (
    "update-ref", "-m", RUN_REFLOG_MESSAGE,
    branch, commit, parent or "",
)
moved = self.git(*update, check=False)
if moved.returncode == 0:
    return commit
parent = RESOLVE_PARENT
<<constants>>=
RUN_REFLOG_MESSAGE = "learnlog: record run"
@

The empty string is how [[update-ref]] is told that the ref must not
exist yet, so the same call covers the first commit on a branch and
every one after it.
Neither command needs the tree budget of
\cref{sec:commit-message-stdin}: [[commit-tree]] writes one object and
[[update-ref]] writes one ref, and neither walks the work tree however
large the project is.

The reflog message is a constant.
It carries no message text, which keeps it clear of the argument-size
limit that put commit messages on standard input, and the commit it
describes says which half of which run it is anyway.

<<repo methods>>=
def branch_tip(self, branch):
    """Return the commit ``branch`` points at, or ``None``."""
    probe = self.git(
        "rev-parse", "--verify", "--quiet", branch, check=False,
    )
    return probe.stdout.strip() or None
@

\subsection{What [[git commit]] did that we now do not}

[[commit-tree]] is plumbing, so it is worth being explicit about what
stops happening around the commit.
Identity is not among it: the same [[user.name]] and [[user.email]]
apply, so a run is still authored by the student
(\cref{sec:init-repo}).
Signing is: [[commit.gpgsign]] is a porcelain setting and plumbing
never consults it.
Initialisation switches signing off in any case, so nothing changes in
practice---but it changes from a setting that could be switched back
into a property of the mechanism.

Message cleanup goes too.
[[git commit]] strips trailing whitespace, collapses runs of blank
lines and drops leading and trailing ones; the trailer sends the
program's captured output through that filter, so a program printing
two blank lines had one recorded.
Verbatim is what a log of program output ought to be, which makes this
the second thing the change improves by accident---the first being the
cherry-picked author date above.
The one thing we add is a closing newline when the message lacks one,
because a commit object without it makes [[git log]] run the following
line into the message.

Finally, the reflog: [[update-ref]] writes one, so the safety net that
kept the lost commits of the detached-head case findable is still
there for the commits that now do not need it.


\section{Beginning a run}
\label{sec:beginning-a-run}

At the start of each program execution, [[begin_run]] snapshots the work
tree and commits it together with the run header.

Whether the code changed is no longer part of that: every run gets its
own header commit (\cref{sec:two-commit-protocol}), so the
[[git diff --cached --quiet]] probe that this section used to describe
as one of the places in this module where a non-zero exit is data
rather than trouble went away with the commit folding it served.
The probe that fills its place is the combined [[HEAD]] lookup of
\cref{sec:head-state}, which also hands [[commit_on_branch]] the
parent it would otherwise read for itself.

<<repo methods>>=
def begin_run(self, script, args, start_time):
    """Record the start of a program run.

    Snapshots the current code state and commits it with the run
    header, which carries the run id that :meth:`finalize_run`
    will refer back to.

    Returns the run header string.
    """
    self.run_id = new_run_id()
    self.run_script = script
    self.run_args = list(args)
    header = format_run_header(
        script, args, start_time, self.run_id,
    )

    with self.commit_lock() as lock:
        <<commit the code state with the run header>>

    return header
@

The commit is always made, including for the common case where the
student re-runs unchanged code: [[commit-tree]] has no notion of an
empty commit to refuse, so the [[--allow-empty]] that used to say so
has nothing left to say.
The tree is the work tree as it looks now, which is the state the
program is about to execute, and [[git write-tree]] turns the staged
index into the object [[commit_on_branch]] needs
(\cref{sec:commit-on-branch}).

<<commit the code state with the run header>>=
branch, tip = self.branch_and_expected_tip()
self.reclaim_run_indexes(lock)
with self.run_index(f"{self.run_id}-begin") as index_path:
    <<seed the private index from the shared one>>
    unfinished = self.unfinished_operation()
    if unfinished:
        <<clear the half-finished operation from the private index>>
    self.git("add", ".", timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT)
    tree = self.git(
        "write-tree", timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
    ).stdout.strip()
    self.commit_on_branch(branch, tree, header, parent=tip)
    if not unfinished:
