\subsection{Indexes a killed run left behind}
\label{sec:reclaim-run-indexes}

The [[finally]] of \cref{sec:private-index} covers a run that dies of
something Python can see.
It covers nothing of a run that is killed outright: [[SIGKILL]] is not
deliverable, the interpreter never reaches another line, and the index
and its lock stay on disk with nobody left to remove them.
That is the case a student actually meets---a program stopped from the
window manager, a terminal closed, a container torn down---so some
later run has to do it instead.

Sweeps like this one live or die on how they decide which files are
finished with, and the usual instruments are guesses.
An age threshold has to name a duration that nothing legitimate
exceeds, and there is none to name here: a seeded index carries the
modification time of the index it was copied from
(\cref{sec:stat-cache}), so its age says nothing at all about the run
holding it.
A naming convention proves only who created a file, never whether they
are done with it.

There is a fact available instead of a guess.
A private index exists only inside a [[run_index]] block; both blocks
in this module---the initial commit (\cref{sec:init-repo}) and the
header commit above---run inside a [[commit_lock]]
(\cref{sec:commit-lock}); and the kernel drops that lock however its
holder dies, killed or not.
A process that holds the commit lock is therefore looking at a
directory in which no [[run_index]] block is live anywhere, so every
file the prefix matches is a leftover.
Nothing has to be timed, and nothing has to be assumed about how the
owner died.

That is a constraint on future call sites as much as an observation
about the present ones.
A [[run_index]] opened outside the commit lock would be a private index
this sweep is entitled to delete while Git is writing it, and the
damage would not be a lost run but a wrong one: [[git write-tree]] over
an index that has gone missing writes the empty tree, and the header
commit would record a project with no files in it.

Which is why the sweep is handed the lock rather than told to take one.
[[_acquire_lock]] answers \enquote{no lock} the same way it answers
every other failure---with [[None]]---so a method that took its own
could not tell holding the lock from having given up on it, and giving
up is precisely when it must not sweep.
Requiring the handle makes the precondition something the caller has to
produce.
Where [[fcntl]] is missing it is [[None]] always, and that platform
keeps its leftovers rather than gambling with them, which is the trade
every other user of this lock makes.

One case is left over: a run that could not take the lock and made an
index anyway.
It is already committing unserialised (\cref{sec:commit-lock}), and it
now also runs without the cover that holding the lock is what conveys.
What answers it is how narrow the case is.
[[_acquire_lock]] comes back empty-handed only where the filesystem
refuses the lock outright---in which case nothing here sweeps either---
or where something is recreating [[learnlog.lock]] faster than
[[LOCK_ATTEMPTS]] tries can catch it, and that is not a repository in
which any of the guarantees hanging off this lock still hold.

<<repo methods>>=
def reclaim_run_indexes(self, lock):
    """Remove private index files that killed runs left behind.

    Does nothing unless ``lock`` is a held commit lock: without
    one, a file found here may belong to a run that is writing
    it right now.
    """
    if lock is None:
        return
    for leftover in sorted(
        self.git_dir.glob(f"{RUN_INDEX_PREFIX}*")
    ):
        try:
            leftover.unlink(missing_ok=True)
        except OSError as error:
            report_error(
                "reclaim run index", error, self.workdir,
            )
@

One glob finds both halves of a pair, because Git appends its suffix to
the index's own name and the prefix is still at the front of the
result.
The shared index and its lock are [[index]] and [[index.lock]], and the
prefix matches neither---the hyphen that made the private names
readable is what keeps them out of reach here.
A file that disappears between the listing and the unlink is not a
failure but a run's own [[finally]] having got there first, which is
why it is asked for with [[missing_ok]] rather than reported.
Anything else is reported and the sweep goes on, so one file that
cannot be removed does not keep the others.

The call sits at the top of the header commit, inside the lock and
before this run makes an index of its own, which is what lets
\enquote{every file the prefix matches} be read without an exception
for ours.

Let us leave behind what a killed run leaves behind---an index and the
lock Git was writing it through---and check that the next run clears
both:

