The property the whole argument rests on needs a second process, since
the lock it turns on is a kernel object shared between processes and
not between threads of one.
The roles are split across the two: here we are the run in progress,
holding the commit lock with a private index of our own on disk, and
there is a second run started into exactly that situation.
A whole run rather than a bare call to the sweep, because where the
call sits is half of what is under test---a sweep moved outside the
lock, or one that stopped asking for it, would still pass a test that
called it correctly by hand.

The child announces itself once its imports are done, which keeps
interpreter startup---by far the most variable thing in this test---out
of the window we measure.
After that a run that swept without the lock would need milliseconds to
take the pair.
So two things are asked at once: that the child is still running a
second later, and that both files are still there.
Then the lock comes off, and the same child is expected to finish and
to have taken both files with it.

