\subsection{Keeping the stat cache warm}
\label{sec:stat-cache}

An index is also a stat cache: Git re-hashes a file only when its
[[stat]] data differs from what the index recorded.
Starting each run from an empty index would therefore re-read the whole
project on every run, so we seed the private index with a copy of the
shared one, and move it back afterwards.
Moving it back keeps the cache warm for the next run and keeps
[[learnlog git status]] honest---an index left behind at repository
initialisation would report every later commit as unstaged work.

The copy has to carry the index's own modification time with it, and
that is not tidiness: it is what keeps the cache from lying.
Git compares file times at whole-second resolution, so an edit that
lands in the same second as the one the index recorded, and happens not
to change the file's size, is invisible to that comparison.
What saves Git is the \emph{racily clean} rule: an entry whose recorded
time is not older than the index's own modification time may have been
written after the index was, so Git re-reads the file instead of
trusting its [[stat]] data.
Since Git writes the index only after it has stat'ed the file, that
rule covers precisely the window the second-resolution comparison is
blind to.

Copying with a plain [[copyfile]] stamps the new index with the present
moment.
Once that moment falls in a later second than the entry's recorded
time---which it does as soon as a second boundary passes between the
run that wrote the cache and the run that reads it---the rule no longer
fires, and the second-resolution comparison is left to answer alone.
A student who edits a file and re-runs it within one second, which is
the ordinary edit--run rhythm this tool exists to record, then gets a
header commit holding the code from \emph{before} the edit.
This is measured, not feared: in a loop that ran, edited by the same
number of bytes, and ran again, [[copyfile]] recorded the stale version
five times in four hundred sequences, and preserving the timestamp
recorded it none in twelve hundred.
[[shutil.copy2]] copies the modification time along with the contents,
which makes the private index behave like the shared one it stands in
for---all the rule ever assumed.

Seeding remains best-effort, but \enquote{best effort} has to mean
\emph{no cache} rather than \emph{a bad one}.
A copy that fails part-way leaves a truncated file Git cannot read at
all, and one whose timestamp did not survive is the silent-wrong-data
case above.
So a failed seeding removes what it made, and [[git add .]] rebuilds
the cache from nothing: that costs one re-read of the project and
nothing else.

<<seed the private index from the shared one>>=
shared_index = self.git_dir / SHARED_INDEX_NAME
try:
    shutil.copy2(shared_index, index_path)
except OSError:
    index_path.unlink(missing_ok=True)
@

Moving the index back is a write to [[.learnlog/index]], and that file
comes with a protocol we were ignoring.
Every Git command that writes the index first creates
[[.learnlog/index.lock]] with [[O_EXCL]], builds the new index there,
and renames it into place; the [[O_EXCL]] is what makes a second writer
fail loudly instead of interleaving.
A bare [[os.replace]] takes part in none of it.
Run alongside a [[learnlog git add]] the student typed, both writers
prepare a whole index out of what they read, and whichever [[rename]]
lands second is the one that survives---the other's work is gone, with
nothing anywhere to say so.

So we take the lock Git would have taken.
Creating [[index.lock]] with [[O_CREAT]] and [[O_EXCL]] \emph{is} the
protocol: holding it is what makes a concurrent Git command fail rather
than interleave, and failing to create it is how a concurrent Git
command tells us it is already writing the index.
Readers take no lock at all, and need none: we still install the file
with a single [[rename]], so a reader sees the old index or the new
one, never a half-written one.

A lock we cannot get means we skip the publish, and that is a choice
rather than a resignation---but only when \enquote{cannot get} means
what we think it means.
[[O_EXCL]] refuses for exactly one reason we planned for, and
[[FileExistsError]] is that reason spelled out: somebody else is
writing the index.
Every other way [[open]] can fail here---a read-only [[.learnlog/]], a
full disk, a directory that has been pulled out from under us---is a
failure this module is not entitled to read as a busy neighbour, and
catching them together made all of them look like one.
Separating them costs one [[except]] clause and buys the difference
between a design decision and a defect (\cref{sec:diagnostics}).
The same holds below for the [[rename]] and for taking the lock file
off again: neither has a benign reading at all, so neither may pass
without a line in the log.
The skip itself stays silent, because a diagnostic for the case we
designed for would only teach whoever reads the log to ignore the file.


What we are publishing is a stat cache and nothing more---the header
commit is already made, out of the private index, and stands whatever
happens here.
Losing the cache costs the next run one [[git add .]] that re-reads the
project, which is what the first run does anyway.
Waiting for the lock would trade that cost for making a student's
program wait on the student's own Git command, and
\cref{sec:commit-lock} has already said what we think of that trade.

The lock file has to come off again in every case, because left behind
it is not our problem but the student's: the next [[git add]] in that
repository fails with \enquote{Unable to create index.lock}, and a
repository that refuses to stage anything is exactly the damage this
tool must never do.
Hence the [[finally]]---and hence that only the process whose
[[O_EXCL]] succeeded removes it.
Removing a lock we found rather than made would hand the index to two
writers at once, which is the one thing the lock is there to prevent.

<<publish the private index as the shared one>>=
shared_lock = self.git_dir / SHARED_INDEX_LOCK_NAME
try:
    lock_fd = os.open(shared_lock, INDEX_LOCK_FLAGS, 0o666)
except FileExistsError:
    pass
except OSError as error:
    report_error("take index lock", error, self.workdir)
else:
    <<move the index into place and drop the lock>>
<<constants>>=
SHARED_INDEX_NAME = "index"
SHARED_INDEX_LOCK_NAME = SHARED_INDEX_NAME + GIT_LOCK_SUFFIX
INDEX_LOCK_FLAGS = os.O_CREAT | os.O_EXCL | os.O_WRONLY
@

<<move the index into place and drop the lock>>=
try:
    os.replace(index_path, shared_index)
except OSError as error:
    report_error("publish run index", error, self.workdir)
finally:
    os.close(lock_fd)
    try:
        shared_lock.unlink()
    except OSError as error:
        report_error(
            "release index lock", error, self.workdir,
        )
