\subsection{Snapshotting around a half-finished operation}

Seeding brings more than the stat cache across when the student is in
the middle of a merge (\cref{sec:unfinished-operations}).
The index a conflicted merge leaves behind holds each conflicted path
in several versions with no decision between them, and
[[git write-tree]]---which is how the staged index becomes the tree we
commit---refuses to write anything at all while that is so.

[[git add .]] would resolve most of it on the way past: staging a file
replaces its unmerged entries with the one version now in the work
tree.
Depending on that is the wrong bet twice over.
It makes the header commit contingent on [[git add]] reaching every
conflicted path, and a path it does not reach costs the run its
header; and it leaves the base of the snapshot to be decided by
accident.

[[read-tree --reset]] settles both.
It is the form that discards unmerged entries instead of failing on
them, and what it reads in is the recording branch's tree, so the
snapshot demonstrably starts from the last thing the log recorded and
[[git add .]] then brings it up to the work tree exactly as it does on
any other run.

The other half is what must \emph{not} happen.
Publishing this index as the shared one would hand the student back an
index in which Git considers their conflict resolved---the same damage
as the merge commit of \cref{sec:unfinished-operations}, done to the
index instead of to the history.
So during a half-finished operation the copy stays private and is
thrown away with the rest, at the price of one cold cache on the next
run, and the merge the student is in the middle of---[[MERGE_HEAD]],
the message, the unmerged index, the work tree---is left as they left
it.

The work tree we do read, and we record what is there: conflict
markers included.
That is the code the program ran, and
\cref{sec:two-commit-protocol}'s answer to which code state belongs to
a run has no exception for code the student is unhappy with.
Recording the pre-merge state instead would be the misleading
option---it would show a run failing on a syntax error in a file that,
as recorded, contains no syntax error.

<<clear the half-finished operation from the private index>>=
self.git(
    "read-tree", "--reset", branch, timeout=GIT_TREE_TIMEOUT,
)
report_error(
    "begin_run",
    UnfinishedOperation(
        f"{unfinished} in {self.git_dir}: recorded the run "
        f"without touching it"
    ),
    self.workdir,
)
@

The diagnostic is the transparency half of the decision: a run that
quietly behaves differently is worse than one that says so, and
[[.learnlog/errors.log]] is where learnlog says the things it must not
say on the student's terminal (\cref{sec:diagnostics}).
That channel renders the exception's type name, which is what a class
of our own buys here: a line naming an unfinished operation rather
than a line naming [[RuntimeError]].
It is never raised.

<<classes>>=
class UnfinishedOperation(RuntimeError):
    """A Git operation was left in progress in the log repository.

    Reported, never raised: a run records itself around a merge,
    cherry-pick, revert or rebase rather than disturbing it.
    """
@

Git parks that state in files whose names are part of its
interface---[[git status]] reads the same ones to tell the student
what is in progress---so asking the filesystem answers the question
without a subprocess at all.

<<repo methods>>=
def unfinished_operation(self):
    """Return the name of a Git operation left in progress.

    ``None`` when the repository is not in the middle of a merge,
    cherry-pick, revert or rebase.
    """
    for name in UNFINISHED_OPERATION_FILES:
        if (self.git_dir / name).exists():
            return name
    return None
<<constants>>=
UNFINISHED_OPERATION_FILES = (
    "MERGE_HEAD",
    "CHERRY_PICK_HEAD",
    "REVERT_HEAD",
    "rebase-merge",
    "rebase-apply",
)
@

