A stat cache that lies is silent, so the seeding above gets a test that
reproduces the lie on purpose.
Three things have to line up for it: the two versions must be the same
size, the edit must land in the same second as the version the index
recorded, and the run that reads the cache must start after that second
has passed---otherwise the racily clean rule fires and the bug hides.
Waiting for a second boundary twice arranges all three.
The first wait gives the write--run--edit sequence a whole fresh second
to fit inside, and the second wait moves the run that reads the cache
out of that second.

<<test functions>>=
def _wait_for_the_next_second():
    """Block until the clock crosses into the next second."""
    deadline = int(time.time()) + 1
    while time.time() < deadline:
        time.sleep(0.005)

def test_begin_run_stages_edit_within_one_second(workdir):
    """A same-size edit inside one time granule is still staged."""
    repo = LearnlogRepo(workdir)
    script = workdir / "hello.py"
    _wait_for_the_next_second()
    script.write_text('print("v1")\n')
    repo.begin_run("hello.py", [], datetime.datetime.now())
    script.write_text('print("v2")\n')
    _wait_for_the_next_second()
    repo.begin_run("hello.py", [], datetime.datetime.now())
    assert repo.git("show", "HEAD:hello.py").stdout \
        == 'print("v2")\n'
@

